    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "TheaterEvent",
      "id": "3052cb6ddaf83b5b",
      "title": "Τα Ομώνυμα Έλκονται - Νικόλας Σμυρνάκης",
      "description": "28 Φεβ - 20 Μαρ Τα Ομώνυμα Έλκονται - Νικόλας Σμυρνάκης Πολλαπλοι χωροι",
      "fullDescription": "The lights come up on a stage that's been set for revelation. You're in one of those theaters where the architecture demands attention, but tonight the focus pulls toward a single figure. Nikolas Smyrnakis prepares to disappear into characters who share more than a name.\n\n\"Likes Attract\" works on a premise that sounds simple until you watch it unfold. Smyrnakis plays homonymous characters — people who share names but nothing else. Or so it seems. As the evening progresses, the connections between these separate lives start surfacing in ways that feel earned rather than forced. The comedy comes from recognition, the drama from what we recognize in ourselves.\n\nSmyrnakis has built a reputation in Greek theater for performances that layer humor over something more unsettling. His timing operates at the edge between laugh and wince. Tonight's material gives him room to shift between personas, each distinct enough to feel like different actors, similar enough to create the evening's central tension. The writing matches the performer — smart without announcing itself.\n\nThe crowd filtering into these multiple venues carries the particular energy of Greek theater audiences. These are people who follow performers across productions, who remember Smyrnakis from earlier work and came specifically for what he brings. Conversations before curtain compare notes on previous shows, establishing the shared context that makes insider references land. By intermission, strangers are debating which character works best.\n\nGreek comedy theater operates differently than its English-language counterpart. The rhythms are faster, the physical comedy broader, the emotional pivots sharper. If you're not fluent in Greek, you'll catch the physicality but miss the wordplay. Smyrnakis builds much of his comedy on language — puns, double meanings, the music of Greek syllables doing their work.\n\nThe venues hosting \"Likes Attract\" have been selected to match the production's scale. Intimate enough that Smyrnakis can play the back row without amplification, large enough to generate the collective energy that comedy requires. When the room laughs together, it laughs as a single organism.\n\nIf you need your theater in English or prefer spectacle over substance, this night will frustrate you. The humor operates in Greek at native speed, no subtitles, no concessions. But if you've been looking for Greek theater that trusts its audience, where the performer earns laughs through craft rather than shortcuts — you find your way to whichever venue is closest.\n\n| Info | Details |\n|------|---------|\n| **Date** | Saturday, February 28, 2026 |\n| **Venues** | Multiple locations |\n| **Language** | Greek (no subtitles) |\n| **Duration** | ~90 minutes |\n| **Tickets** | Check venue listings |\n\nSmyrnakis playing multiple characters who share a name — theater that finds the comedy in coincidence and the meaning in connection.",
      "startDate": "2026-02-28T20:00:00+03:00",
      "endDate": null,
